t destination/port. node3 successfully curled http://10.100.24.10:8080/ and received the app's response both before and after a full reboot cycle of node1 and node2, confirming the published path is live and durable across a restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2d849b700bac408b\",\"cmd-129e1ee6e4d744f5\",\"cmd-3e85a4813de14566\",\"cmd-d2100de8b49e446f\",\"cmd-b31c36dee0084ab9\",\"cmd-e15d8d8b17504936\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-no-broader-exposure\",\"requirement\":\"The rest of the private (lan) network between node1 and node2 must remain closed to node3 (only the single published port should be reachable).\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The VyOS forward-filter default-action was set to drop, with only rule 10 (destination 10.50.50.2:8080) and an established/related rule permitted. node3's direct attempts to reach the private-network address (http://10.50.50.2:8080 and http://10.50.50.1) both failed (connection timed out / no route to host) both before and after the node1/node2 reboot cycle, while the published port continued to work, demonstrating the private network stayed closed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-757f20c4d1e34531\",\"cmd-3748732cc5074c95\",\"cmd-2d849b700bac408b\",\"cmd-129e1ee6e4d744f5\",\"cmd-9bb2c2223fa643ce\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"The publishing solution must be implemented using Ansible.\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All configuration (VyOS NAT/firewall via vyos.vyos.vyos_config, node2 NIC/routing via nmcli/ip route modules, and node2 app/service/firewalld setup) was driven from a single Ansible playbook (/root/port-forward-ansible/playbook.yml) run repeatedly via ansible-playbook against a dedicated inventory, with the final successful run showing all tasks converged (ok/changed, no failures) and the outcome independently verified from node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2bf4152c0c444452\",\"cmd-63dafa4351a743a7\",\"cmd-3ff5b1a950af4f2a\",\"cmd-dc6d9c947c7442f2\"]}],\"confidence\":0.82,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.55,\"summary\":\"Most exploratory/diagnostic commands were read-only or fully reverted (e.g., a manual `ip rule`/`ip route table 100` policy-routing experiment on node2 was added then completely removed via `ip rule del`/`ip route flush`/`nmcli` cleanup before the equivalent change was made persistently through the playbook). Necessary but heavy prerequisite work was required on node2 to get a Python interpreter Ansible could use (RHEL7 shipped only Python 2/3.6, and ansible-core 2.20 requires 3.9+): after unsuccessful attempts via EPEL python36 and SCL rh-python38 (3.8, still too old), the executor built and installed Python 3.11 from source. This was a legitimate, well-justified prerequisite, and temporary repo files and the source tree were cleaned up afterward, but the intermediate rh-python38 package/runtime and 'Development Tools' toolchain installed along the way were left in place unused after the interpreter path was switched to /usr/local/bin/python3.11, adding unnecessary residual package footprint on node2. On node1, the executor also added a public key to root's authorized_keys (in addition to the vyos user's key) while diagnosing an unrelated SSH issue; root login remained denied afterward and this credential file was never removed, leaving an unused but security-relevant residual credential.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1f5ba740a9284ed6\",\"cmd-9f06559189f84371\",\"cmd-f9b78af068804c52\",\"cmd-a9b1dc2acb9b49d8\",\"cmd-e4a2a5d3995144f3\",\"cmd-b846f4456e5e4318\",\"cmd-51eb65af1ae44dcd\",\"cmd-35fc23b9929541d2\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Despite the executor's own final report claiming the run was 'blocked' by a cluster lease expiry, the captured command trace shows the port-forwarding task was actually completed and verified: an Ansible playbook configured VyOS DNAT plus a default-drop forward firewall on node1 exposing only node2:8080, node3 could reach the app through node1's public IP while direct access to the private 10.50.50.0/24